<article class="env-definition" id="0486">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0486">Definition <span data-tag="0486">66.19.1</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
A <em>geometric point</em> of $X$ is a morphism $\overline{x} : \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k) \to X$, where $k$ is an algebraically closed field. We often abuse notation and write $\overline{x} = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For every geometric point $\overline{x}$ we have the corresponding “image” point $x \in |X|$. We say that $\overline{x}$ is a <em>geometric point lying over $x$</em>. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>